---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CompressImageToTarget from '@/components/tools/image-edit/CompressImageToTarget.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="compress-image-to-20kb">
  <CompressImageToTarget client:load targetKb={20} inputLabel="Drop the photo, signature or scan that must fit 20 KB" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>20 KB is a very small budget</h2>
    <p>
      For scale: this sentence as plain text is about 80 bytes. Twenty kilobytes is 20,480 bytes, and a
      photograph must encode all visible detail in that. Form-processing systems built for the storage
      of the dial-up era set this limit, mostly exam boards, recruitment portals and government
      e-services. You can reach it, but you must know what to give up.
    </p>

    <h2>What fits</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Content</th><th>Realistic at 20 KB</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Scanned signature on white</td><td>Easy, even at 800 px wide</td><td>Mostly flat white; compresses very well</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Passport-style headshot, plain wall</td><td>300 × 400 px, quality 55–70</td><td>The intended use of most 20 KB limits</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Document scan, text only</td><td>Legible up to about 900 px wide</td><td>Convert it to grayscale first if the tool allows</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Outdoor snapshot</td><td>250 × 350 px, visibly soft</td><td>Crop tightly or accept the softness</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Screenshot with fine text</td><td>Not really</td><td>Text needs sharp edges that JPEG cannot give at this size</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>The single most effective move: crop</h2>
    <p>
      At this size, the problem for the encoder is the pixel count. A 3000 × 4000 photo has 12 million
      pixels; a crop to the head and shoulders can leave 600,000. That is twenty times fewer pixels to
      encode, which gives much more quality at the same byte count. Crop first with
      <a href="/crop-image">Crop Image</a>, then come back here.
    </p>

    <h2>How the search behaves at extreme targets</h2>
    <p>
      The tool first does a binary search of the JPEG quality at full resolution. At a 20 KB target this
      usually fails even at quality 5, so the tool starts the second pass: it searches the scale factor,
      resamples with a Lanczos3 filter, and encodes again at a fixed mid quality. A small image encoded
      cleanly looks much better than a full-size image smeared into blocks, so the tool fixes quality
      and searches size. The result card lists each attempt and shows the final values.
    </p>

    <h2>Format matters more than usual here</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>WebP keeps about a third more detail than JPEG at 20 KB. If the portal accepts <code>.webp</code>, use it.</li>
      <li>JPEG is the safe default, and almost all forms expect it.</li>
      <li>PNG is a poor choice for photos here: it is lossless, so only the size can change, and a 20 KB PNG photo is tiny. It is the best choice for a signature or a line drawing, which compress losslessly to almost nothing.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Common 20 KB requirements</h2>
    <p>
      Indian competitive-exam portals are the best-known source: a photograph between 20 and 50 KB and a
      signature between 10 and 20 KB, plus fixed pixel dimensions. When a range is given, aim for the
      top of it, because the largest allowed file is the clearest. When pixel dimensions are also
      specified, set the width limit first so the search stays within them.
    </p>

    <h2>What not to do</h2>
    <p>
      Do not put the output through the tool a second time. Each JPEG generation quantises coefficients
      that were already rounded, so errors add up quickly. A file compressed twice to 20 KB looks much
      worse than the same original compressed once. If the result is not good enough, go back to your
      highest-quality original, crop more, and try again.
    </p>

    <h2>Other targets</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/compress-image-to-50kb">50 KB</a> ·
      <a href="/compress-image-to-100kb">100 KB</a> ·
      <a href="/compress-image-to-200kb">200 KB</a> ·
      <a href="/reduce-image-size-in-kb">any target you type</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
